  <h2 id="why">Why PCB career salary numbers online feel so confusing</h2>

  <p>
    Every source seems to quote a different number for the same career.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is usually because the numbers are not measuring the same thing.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">The usual bad advice</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>"Doctors earn X lakhs," as if one number covers an intern and a senior consultant equally.</li>
      <li>"Government jobs pay less, full stop," ignoring NPA, DA, HRA, and pension-linked value.</li>
      <li>"Nursing/pharmacy/physiotherapy don't pay well," treated as a permanent, blanket fact.</li>
      <li>"Biotech means a fixed lab-job salary," ignoring how much the specific role changes the number.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout__footer">
      None of this tells you what your actual first offer will look like, or how fast that number can realistically move.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    A PCB salary depends on four things: the exact role (not just the degree), government or private employer, the city and employer type, and years plus certification.
