  <h2 id="not-only-salary">Why salary should not be the only decision factor</h2>

  <p>
    A high number on a table does not tell you whether you can do the work every day.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      A Rs 20-30 LPA senior figure, ten years out, is not a reason to ignore whether you can handle night shifts, heavy documentation, patient loss, or years of modest pay before the real income starts.
    </p>
    <p>
      Licensing, time to income, stamina, and the actual daily reality of a path matter as much as the title on the salary table.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    If you have not tested fit yet, run your shortlist through the 4-Checkpoint Protocol in <a href="/blog/career-options/career-options-after-12th-pcb/">career options after 12th PCB</a> before letting a salary number make the decision for you.
  </p>

  <p class="note-line">
    <strong>Salary is one input, not the whole answer.</strong> The strongest long-term outcome usually comes from a route where fit, skill-building, and visible proof of work stack up together — that combination is what actually compounds toward earlier financial freedom, not the highest number on a table in isolation.
  </p>

